{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_kgwzxp65mqywa22rciaspbt7vla4vc6kxt4p4ubmn2wo5rfiatda","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the code is\n\nThe entire C library is 5 lines of logic: `helloworld()` does `printf(\"Hello World!\\n\"); return 0;`, and `main()` returns `helloworld()`. Zero branches, zero parameters, zero validation, zero `#ifdef` — I confirmed this mechanically by grepping every non-comment line.\n\n## Phase A — surface mapped\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` declares only an *executable*, so I built the `.so` out-of-tree from the same two translation units (`c_src/` is byte-for-byte untouched — still just its 4 original files). Added a `[lib] crate-type = [\"cdylib\",\"rlib\"]` target with `#[no_mangle]` wrappers.\n\n- **SYMBOLS.md** — C exports exactly `helloworld` and `main`; both exported by the Rust `.so`, symbol diff **empty**, no undefined non-libc symbols. Nothing stubbed.\n- **ERRORS.md** — 18 rows. Since the C rejects nothing, rows derive from its *real* failure mode (the discarded `printf` result under ENOSPC/EBADF/EPIPE) plus every mandated FFI boundary: null/garbage pointers, zero & oversized lengths, out-of-range enum ints, extra args through the unprototyped K&R declarator.\n- **CONFIGS.md** — 25 rows across the axes the code actually interacts with: entry point (`helloworld` low-level, `main`, whole program), fd-1 destination, all three `setvbuf` modes, empty/one/many calls, interleaving with foreign writers, concurrency, flush discipline, process-lifetime.\n\n## Phases B/C — 42 differential tests\n\nBoth libraries are `dlopen`ed and called through `dlsym` only (never a direct Rust call). Each scenario runs in a `fork()`ed child for isolated stdio state, with randomized inputs from fixed seeds.\n\n## Two real divergences found and fixed\n\n1. **Buffering/flush timing.** The translation wrote via `std::io::stdout()` (line-buffered, flushes eagerly); C writes via `FILE *stdout` (fully buffered on a file/pipe). Same bytes, different *timing* — visible as reordered output when interleaved with any other fd-1 writer, and as output that C would have lost if killed before exit. `src/sillymain.rs` now calls the platform `printf`, sharing the same stream.\n2. **`SIGPIPE` disposition.** Rust's runtime installs `SIG_IGN` before `main`; C does not. With stdout on a reader-less pipe, the C program is killed by signal 13 while the Rust binary exited 0. `src/main.rs` now restores `SIG_DFL` first.\n\n## Phase D — gate closed\n\nCargo.toml has no `[features]`, so the one valid combination is the empty one; `verify.sh` derives that from the manifest and also runs the default and `--all-features` builds, plus debug/release Rust × default/`-O2` C — 4 test configurations, all 42 tests green, symbol parity checked per profile.\n\nI also added `negative_control.sh`, which builds 7 deliberately wrong translations and requires the suite to reject each. It initially caught a genuine hole in my own tests: a mutant that *propagated* `printf`'s error survived, because under default buffering `printf` never fails at call time. I added `_IONBF`/`_IOLBF`/`_IOFBF(8)` sub-cases to the error rows; now all 7 mutants are caught and the real translation passes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1810,"cache_read_input_tokens":195643,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1810,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1c5b48b8-39c1-47d7-8198-6b86fae5a6f6","uuid":"89c249ba-5413-4519-bb22-04f087ff7257","timestamp":"2026-08-17T21:58:53.672Z"}
